Closes #110237

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where users upgrading from `2026.7.2-beta.1` could have `openclaw doctor --fix` abort while migrating the shared state database to SQLite STRICT tables after a Gateway restart handoff recorded fractional trace timestamps.

## Why This Change Was Made

Restart-trace timestamps are now canonicalized to integer milliseconds at the restart-handoff persistence boundary. During the existing schema-v2-to-v3 transition, doctor prunes expired transient handoffs and converts only known REAL trace cells on live handoffs before the generic STRICT-table rebuild.

The generic STRICT migrator remains lossless and table-agnostic. The repair is owned by the restart-handoff/state-schema boundary, and the SQLite schema version is unchanged.

## User Impact

Affected upgrades can complete without manual SQLite repair. Expired restart handoffs are discarded as transient state, while valid live handoffs are preserved with canonical integer timing values. Future packaged Gateways write integral timestamps directly into the STRICT table.

## Evidence

- Final rebased head: `cda2ea3222c847176b06f70e8979e50655365ab1`, based on `e5b2ca3c6bd1fdd44468f05325af7f75c831d89d`.
- Focused and adjacent regression coverage: 107 tests passed across `src/infra/restart-handoff.test.ts`, `src/state/openclaw-state-db-restart-handoff-migration.test.ts`, and `src/state/openclaw-state-db.test.ts`.
- Full `pnpm check:changed` passed, including formatting, core/core-test typechecks, focused lint, import-cycle checks, and SQLite/storage guards.
- Fresh Codex autoreview: no findings; patch correct at 0.96 confidence.
- [Direct AWS Crabbox package-upgrade proof](https://crabbox.openclaw.ai/portal/runs/run_ffc29a0e9466) (`provider=aws`, lease `cbx_fbb0896dc172`, run `run_ffc29a0e9466`):
  - verified the synced changed-file digest and built the candidate with head provenance `cda2ea3`;
  - exact packaged beta.1 (`a911e58`) ran a real Gateway, reached `/readyz`, and exited through its supervised SIGUSR1 restart path, which persisted live REAL restart-trace cells in the schema-v2 non-STRICT table;
  - exact packaged beta.2 (`54d98a4`) reproduced the REAL-to-INTEGER STRICT migration failure and atomic rollback to schema version 2; the diagnostic copy removed only the unrelated shipped `operator_approvals` blocker before doctor and did not alter the handoff row;
  - an untouched copy of the beta.1 state was upgraded to the packed candidate with zero SQL writes before doctor; doctor succeeded, reached `user_version=5` and schema metadata version 5, made `gateway_restart_handoff` STRICT, retained the live handoff with floored INTEGER trace values, passed `integrity_check`, and had zero foreign-key violations;
  - a second candidate doctor run succeeded, proving migration idempotence;
  - the packaged candidate exercised the same supervised restart path and wrote a new handoff whose trace cells were INTEGER in the STRICT table;
  - the candidate reached `/readyz` after an in-process restart, and CLI/Gateway/RPC all reported `2026.7.2`;
  - the owned AWS lease was released after the run.

AI-assisted with Codex. The implementation, migration policy, regression coverage, and package-level upgrade evidence were reviewed by the author.

## What Problem This Solves

Partially addresses #102313.

Under a non-default per-peer DM session scope, two Matrix users whose MXIDs differ only in case resolve to the same DM session key, so the second user loads and reads the first user's private DM transcript with the bot.

Matrix MXIDs are opaque and case-sensitive. The direct branch of the session-key builder force-lowercased the peer id before building the key, so `@Alice:hs.example` and `@alice:hs.example` collapsed onto one key and one persisted transcript. The group/room path does not have this problem: #75670 / #87366 added case preservation, but only for `channel`/`group` peer kinds, and explicitly left direct peers folded.

Exposure needs two preconditions: an operator opted into a non-default `session.dmScope`, and a homeserver that permits case-distinct MXIDs as distinct users (standard Synapse canonicalizes localparts at registration, so it does not fire there).

## Why This Change Was Made

`src/routing/session-key.ts` direct branch, before:

```ts
peerId = normalizeLowercaseStringOrEmpty(peerId);
```

The group branch of the same function instead routes peer ids through `normalizeSessionPeerId`, which honors the `CASE_PRESERVING_PEERS` registry in `src/sessions/session-key-utils.ts`. After:

```ts
peerId = normalizeSessionPeerId({
  channel: params.channel,
  peerKind: "direct",
  peerId,
});
```

plus enrolling `direct` in the Matrix descriptor. That alone is not sufficient, because the registry drives two layers and `normalizeStoreSessionKey` re-folds the key on every session-store lookup. Two further surfaces were required:

1. **Account-scoped DM shape.** Store canonicalization only matched a `<channel>:<peerKind>:` head, so `agent:<id>:matrix:<account>:direct:<peer>` never matched and still folded. Its span regex now tolerates the account segment, matching how `parseSessionDeliveryRoute` already disambiguates that shape.

2. **Alias proof target.** Folded-alias proof compared the session key against *room* delivery targets. A DM key embeds the peer MXID, not the room it was delivered in, so a room target can never appear in it. Left unchanged, this rejected every exact Matrix DM entry and broke DM session continuity outright (caught by `extensions/matrix/src/matrix/monitor/handler.test.ts`). DM keys are now proven against peer identity (`origin.from` / `origin.nativeDirectUserId`) instead, which is also what keeps a genuinely case-distinct sibling from being adopted as a legacy alias.

3. **Alias proof must recognize the account-scoped DM key.** `requiresFoldedSessionKeyAliasProof` read the peer kind positionally as the segment right after the channel, so `agent:<id>:matrix:<account>:direct:<peer>` resolved no descriptor and reported that no proof was needed. The folded alias was then adopted permissively, so under `per-account-channel-peer` an arriving `@Alice` both read `@alice`'s private transcript and had `@alice`'s session key handed back as a legacy key to delete. It now resolves the descriptor through the same account-scoped `direct`/`dm` shape `parseSessionDeliveryRoute` already disambiguates.

### Why this is the right boundary

The case policy stays in the one registry that already owns it; the direct branch stops being the odd path out. Sibling surfaces:

- **Signal** is the other enrolled channel and is deliberately *not* changed here. Per the maintainer question on #102313, Signal direct ids need their case-sensitivity contract proven first; a regression test pins Signal direct as folded.
- **Case-insensitive channels** (Telegram, Discord numeric ids) are correct to fold and keep folding; a regression test pins that.
- **Legacy lowercased DM rows** are adopted on upgrade via the existing folded-alias machinery, now given the correct proof target and taught the account-scoped key shape, so a returning peer keeps their transcript while a case-distinct sibling's row is neither adopted nor deleted. Covered across both DM scopes and both case directions.

### Known limitation

`per-peer` keys are `agent:<id>:direct:<peerId>` and are channel-agnostic by design ("isolate by sender, across channels"), so they carry no channel to enroll and cannot preserve case without either changing a persisted key shape or changing folding for every channel. `per-peer` therefore still folds; `per-channel-peer` (the documented recommended value) and `per-account-channel-peer` are fixed. This is documented in `docs/concepts/session.md` and a regression test pins the current behavior.

## User Impact

Operators using `session.dmScope: per-channel-peer` or `per-account-channel-peer` on Matrix no longer risk one peer's DM transcript being served to a different, case-distinct peer. No config change is needed and existing DM sessions are preserved. Behavior on every other channel, on the default `main` scope, and on the group/room path is unchanged.

## Evidence

### Verification

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/sessions/session-key-case-preservation.test.ts` — 60 passed. The new cases fail on pristine main; the negative controls that pin Signal/Telegram folding pass on both, as intended. Reverting only the account-scoped alias-proof resolution fails exactly the two account-scoped cases and nothing else.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/routing src/config/sessions src/sessions` — 1675 passed.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/matrix/src/matrix/monitor/handler.test.ts` — 121 passed, including the DM-collision case that caught the continuity regression described above.
- `oxlint` and `oxfmt --check` clean on all changed files; `run-tsgo.mjs -p tsconfig.core.json` reports no errors in any changed file.

## Real behavior proof
Behavior addressed: under a per-peer Matrix DM scope, two case-distinct MXIDs share one session key, so the second peer loads the first peer's persisted DM transcript.
Real environment tested: the production `buildAgentSessionKey` and `resolveSessionStoreEntry` were driven directly, first on pristine base b319493a52 and then on the patched tree at 9c2f5eff14, with identical inputs. Nothing was faked: the real key builder, the real store canonicalizer, and the real folded-alias resolver all ran, over an in-memory session store holding realistic Matrix DM metadata (delivery target is the DM room, peer MXID on origin.from). No external seam was stubbed; this path performs no network calls.
Exact steps or command run after this patch: for each of dmScope `per-channel-peer` and `per-account-channel-peer`, build the DM session keys for `@Alice:hs.example` and `@alice:hs.example`, then resolve the store entry three ways on identical inputs: a store holding only `@Alice`'s session with an incoming `@alice` turn, the mirror (a store holding only `@alice`'s session with an incoming `@Alice` turn), and a store holding only `@Alice`'s own lowercased legacy row with an incoming `@Alice` turn. Report which transcript is handed over, whether the other peer's key is returned for deletion, and whether the peer keeps their own legacy row.
Evidence after fix:
```
# BEFORE (pristine base b319493a52)
dmScope=per-channel-peer
  @Alice key: agent:main:matrix:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice key: agent:main:matrix:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice loads: Alice-private-transcript
  @alice reads @Alice's private DM history: true
  @Alice loads: alice-private-transcript
  @Alice reads @alice's private DM history: true
  @Alice keeps own legacy row on upgrade: Alice-legacy-transcript

dmScope=per-account-channel-peer
  @Alice key: agent:main:matrix:acct1:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice key: agent:main:matrix:acct1:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice loads: Alice-private-transcript
  @alice reads @Alice's private DM history: true
  @Alice loads: alice-private-transcript
  @Alice reads @alice's private DM history: true
  @Alice keeps own legacy row on upgrade: Alice-legacy-transcript

# AFTER (this patch 9c2f5eff14, identical inputs)
dmScope=per-channel-peer
  @Alice key: agent:main:matrix:direct:@Alice:hs.example
  @alice key: agent:main:matrix:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice loads: (none - new session)
  @alice reads @Alice's private DM history: false
  @Alice's session marked for deletion: false
  @Alice loads: (none - new session)
  @Alice reads @alice's private DM history: false
  @alice's session marked for deletion: false
  @Alice keeps own legacy row on upgrade: Alice-legacy-transcript

dmScope=per-account-channel-peer
  @Alice key: agent:main:matrix:acct1:direct:@Alice:hs.example
  @alice key: agent:main:matrix:acct1:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice loads: (none - new session)
  @alice reads @Alice's private DM history: false
  @Alice's session marked for deletion: false
  @Alice loads: (none - new session)
  @Alice reads @alice's private DM history: false
  @alice's session marked for deletion: false
  @Alice keeps own legacy row on upgrade: Alice-legacy-transcript
```
Observed result after fix: on the pristine base both MXIDs collapse onto one identical DM session key and each peer is handed the other's private transcript in both directions and under both scopes. With the patch every one of those reads is refused, the two peers get distinct keys and each starts a clean session of their own, neither peer's session is returned for deletion, and a returning peer still resolves to their own prior lowercased row. An earlier revision of this branch closed only the first direction under `per-account-channel-peer`; that gap is what the account-scoped alias-proof resolution above fixes, and the mirror-direction line is the one that moves.
What was not tested: no live homeserver was staged, so the full inbound-to-transcript-load flow was not exercised end to end against a real Matrix server; the proof drives the real key builder and the real store resolver that decide which transcript loads. The `per-peer` scope is out of scope by design and still folds. Signal direct ids are unchanged pending their identity-contract proof. A full packaged build was not run, as no dynamic-import, packaging, or published surface changed.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where probing a Tlon account could leave the unread name response stream open after the account check returned.

## Why This Change Was Made

The Tlon account probe now cancels an unread `/~/name` response body before releasing its guarded dispatcher. Cancellation remains best-effort so dispatcher cleanup still runs if the response stream rejects cancellation.

## User Impact

Tlon account checks release the response stream and guarded network resources even when an Urbit name endpoint sends a streaming or otherwise unfinished body.

## Evidence

- Real-path loopback proof: `pnpm exec tsx proof-live.ts` imported the production `probeTlonAccount` entrypoint against a localhost Urbit boundary with an unfinished `/~/name` body. On the pinned base, both the 200 and 503 streams remained open after the probe returned; with this change, both streams closed while preserving their results.
- Focused test: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/tlon/src/channel.runtime.test.ts` passes 3 tests covering successful and non-2xx responses plus cancellation failure cleanup.

```text
$ pnpm exec tsx proof-live.ts
base 200: {"result":{"ok":true},"responseClosedBeforeTeardown":false}
base 503: {"result":{"ok":false,"error":"Name request failed: 503"},"responseClosedBeforeTeardown":false}
fix  200: {"result":{"ok":true},"responseClosedBeforeTeardown":true}
fix  503: {"result":{"ok":false,"error":"Name request failed: 503"},"responseClosedBeforeTeardown":true}
```

<details>
<summary>proof-live.ts</summary>

```ts
import http from "node:http";
import path from "node:path";
import { pathToFileURL } from "node:url";

async function runProbe(
  probeTlonAccount: (account: never) => Promise<unknown>,
  status: number,
) {
  let nameResponseClosed = false;
  let resolveNameResponseClosed: (() => void) | undefined;
  const nameResponseClosedPromise = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
    resolveNameResponseClosed = resolve;
  });

  const server = http.createServer((request, response) => {
    if (request.url === "/~/login") {
      response.writeHead(200, {
        "content-type": "text/plain",
        "set-cookie": "urbauth-~zod=proof-cookie",
      });
      response.end("ok");
      return;
    }

    if (request.url === "/~/name") {
      response.writeHead(status, { "content-type": "text/plain" });
      response.write("stream remains open");
      const heartbeat = setInterval(() => response.write("."), 100);
      response.on("close", () => {
        clearInterval(heartbeat);
        nameResponseClosed = true;
        resolveNameResponseClosed?.();
      });
      return;
    }

    response.writeHead(404).end();
  });

  await new Promise<void>((resolve) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
  const address = server.address();
  if (!address || typeof address === "string") {
    throw new Error("loopback server did not expose a TCP port");
  }

  const probePromise = probeTlonAccount({
    accountId: "default",
    configured: true,
    ship: "~zod",
    url: `http://127.0.0.1:${address.port}`,
    code: "proof-code",
    dangerouslyAllowPrivateNetwork: true,
  } as never);

  const outcome = await Promise.race([
    probePromise.then((result: unknown) => ({ kind: "returned" as const, result })),
    new Promise<{ kind: "timeout" }>((resolve) =>
      setTimeout(() => resolve({ kind: "timeout" }), 1_000),
    ),
  ]);

  if (outcome.kind === "timeout") {
    server.closeAllConnections();
    await probePromise;
  } else {
    await Promise.race([
      nameResponseClosedPromise,
      new Promise<void>((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 500)),
    ]);
  }

  const responseClosedBeforeTeardown = nameResponseClosed;
  console.log(
    JSON.stringify({
      status,
      outcome: outcome.kind,
      result: outcome.kind === "returned" ? outcome.result : undefined,
      responseClosedBeforeTeardown,
    }),
  );

  server.closeAllConnections();
  await new Promise<void>((resolve, reject) => {
    server.close((error) => (error ? reject(error) : resolve()));
  });
}

async function main() {
  const runtimeUrl = pathToFileURL(
    path.resolve(process.cwd(), "extensions/tlon/src/channel.runtime.ts"),
  ).href;
  const { probeTlonAccount } = await import(runtimeUrl);

  console.log("entrypoint: probeTlonAccount");
  console.log("dependency-boundary: localhost Urbit HTTP server with an unfinished /~/name body");
  await runProbe(probeTlonAccount, 200);
  await runProbe(probeTlonAccount, 503);
}

void main().catch((error) => {
  console.error(error);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

</details>

